// A theorem about an array: after completion, no entry is undecided.
//
// This is the example the README builds towards, and a miniature of the one
// property that made verification worth doing in the original: a partial
// assignment is completed and then used to index another array *without an
// application-level check*, so a surviving sentinel is not a wrong answer. It
// is a call that traps instead of returning one.
//
// Note the scope. What is proved here is that no entry is the sentinel. That
// the entries are *valid indices* is a second property, over their range, and
// this package does not carry it. Both are needed before the downstream read
// is actually safe; only the first is demonstrated.
//
// Everything here is integer. That is not incidental: MoonBit's verifier has
// no model of `Double`, and a function carrying a contract may not contain
// floating-point operations at all. The split this package demonstrates is
// what makes the technique applicable to a numeric algorithm at all: put the
// index arithmetic where the solver can see it, and leave the arithmetic that
// decides *values* outside.
options(
  "proof-enabled": true,
)
